          "text": "{\"persuasion\":\"PERSUADED\",\"support_scope\":\"FULL\",\"rationale\":\"The strongest alternative—mountain as a place of divine presence, revelation, and worship—describes an important biblical motif, but it does not explain the entry’s cited symbolic cases as directly as the book’s political-authority reading. Daniel’s interpreted dream identifies the great mountain with God’s everlasting kingdom, while Jeremiah addresses imperial Babylon as a destroying mountain. Revelation 17:9-10 further provides an explicit mountain-to-kings correspondence. These texts support kingdom, nation, and ruling authority as a coherent symbolic sense. “High place” and the Valley opposition are also supported by the recurring vertical contrast between exalted powers and the lowly, especially Isaiah 40:4. This does not establish that every biblical mountain has this meaning, but the exact glossary entry does not explicitly claim exclusivity or deny literal mountains and divine-encounter settings.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A mountain symbolizes a kingdom, nation, or organized power under ruling authority.\",\"book_secondary_elaborations\":[\"A mountain is a high place or seat of ruling authority.\",\"Valley is the symbolic opposite of Mountain.\"],\"unsupported_glossary_assertions\":[],\"chapter_only_objections\":[\"The chapter’s claim that Scripture uses mountain reliably and consistently for nation or kingdom overstates the evidence; mountains are plainly polyvalent across biblical genres.\",\"The readings of Revelation 8:8, the mountain-moving sayings, Psalm 46, Joel, and the command to flee to the mountains depend on additional symbolic identifications that are not independently established here.\",\"Reading flight to the mountains as dispersion among nations is less natural than the geographical-refuge reading in its immediate setting.\",\"Reading Satan’s high mountain as a world-ruling kingdom is unnecessary; the narrative can use an extraordinary vantage point without encoding a kingdom.\",\"The chapter’s detailed identifications of sea, trees, animals, rocks, islands, hills, and valleys go beyond what the printed glossary entry asserts.\"],\"strongest_counter_interpretation\":\"Mountain primarily represents the holy sphere of divine presence, revelation, covenant, worship, and encounter, as exemplified by Sinai, Horeb, Zion, the Transfiguration, and Hebrews’ contrast between Sinai and heavenly Zion, rather than political authority as its governing symbolic meaning.\",\"counter_core_identification\":\"A mountain symbolizes an elevated holy place where God is present and reveals himself.\",\"counter_relation_to_book_core\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"material_core_denial\":\"The counter denies that kingdom, nation, or ruling authority is the best governing identification of the symbolic mountain in the evidence selected for the entry.\",\"counter_case\":[\"Sinai and Horeb repeatedly function as settings of theophany, covenant, law, and prophetic encounter.\",\"Zion is explicitly God’s holy mountain and the location of his house, instruction, worship, and royal presence.\",\"The Gospel mountain scenes, especially the Transfiguration, continue the revelation and divine-presence motif.\",\"Hebrews 12 organizes its covenantal contrast around Sinai and Zion as two spheres of approach to God.\",\"This interpretation accounts for a wider canonical motif than a strictly political definition and better cautions against treating all mountains as kingdoms.\"],\"book_explanatory_advantages\":[\"Daniel 2 supplies an interpreted vision: the stone becomes a mountain, and the interpretation identifies the resulting dominion as God’s everlasting kingdom.\",\"Jeremiah’s destroying mountain is imperial Babylon, directly joining mountain imagery to a nation and world-dominating power.\",\"Revelation 17:9-10 explicitly correlates seven mountains with seven kings, strongly confirming the authority association.\",\"The entry unifies divine and hostile mountains as competing political or governmental powers rather than restricting the symbol to holy encounters.\",\"The vertical imagery of height, exaltation, and being brought low naturally supports the entry’s ruling-authority and Valley-opposition language.\"],\"counter_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It accounts naturally for Sinai, Horeb, Zion, the Transfiguration, and other mountain theophanies without converting narrative geography into political symbolism.\",\"It captures holiness, transcendence, worship, and revelation, which the political definition alone does not express.\",\"It better reflects the contextual and polyvalent use of mountain throughout Scripture.\"],\"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\":[],\"comparative_winner\":\"BOOK\",\"decisive_sources\":[\"Daniel 2:35, 44-45\",\"Jeremiah 51:25 in its Babylon context\",\"Revelation 17:9-10\",\"Isaiah 2:2-4\",\"Isaiah 40:4\"],\"unresolved_objections\":[\"The supplied evidence supports kingdom and authority as a genuine symbolic sense, but not as an exclusive meaning for every biblical occurrence.\",\"Many prominent mountain passages concern literal locations of divine encounter and therefore cannot simply be decoded as nations or kingdoms.\",\"The Valley opposition is supported chiefly by vertical and social contrast rather than by an equally explicit inspired definition.\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
        }
